A reviewer claims this maintainability issue:
  title: String concatenation in raw SQL query is an SQL-injection footgun
  lines: 4-4 (new file)
  rationale: Even though `table` is currently a literal, the pattern `db.raw('SELECT * FROM ' + table)` teaches readers and future contributors that concatenation into raw queries is acceptable. Any future change that makes `table` dynamic will silently introduce an injection vulnerability. The pattern itself is a maintainability hazard.
Selecting all columns means callers receive an undocumented, untyped set of fields. Any column added, removed, or renamed in the `users` table silently changes what `all()` returns, breaking callers in ways that are hard to detect statically.

The ONLY context you may use is this hunk:
File: reports.ts
Hunk: @@ -1,1 +1,4 @@
Lines show NEW-file line numbers; + added, - removed, space unchanged.
   1   class Reports {
   2 +   all() {
   3 +     const table = "users";
   4 +     return this.db.raw("SELECT * FROM " + table);
   5 +   }

Is the finding real and supported by THIS hunk? Try to refute it before agreeing. Report your outcome (upheld/refuted), your confidence, and your reasoning.
